# Generic wording in AI artifacts

## Pyramid Layer

- Layer: L0 authoring rule.

## Use This When

- Keep this rule active when writing or editing AI-facing artifacts.
- Apply it to `.cursor/rules`, `.claude/skills`, `AGENTS.md`, `.claude/CLAUDE.md`, prompt files, smoke-test docs, and related agent-context documentation.

## Stop Here If

- Stop once the wording is generic and non-branded.
- Do not apply this rule to runtime config, real filesystem paths, or product documentation unless the task explicitly asks for that.

## Descend To

- No sibling descent required. Apply this rule directly when editing AI artifacts.

## Core rule

- Do **not** anchor AI artifacts to the current project name when a generic phrase works.
- Prefer neutral wording such as:
  - `this project`
  - `the repository`
  - `the server app`
  - `the MCP server`
  - `the ingestion worker`
- Avoid branded examples like project-specific hostnames, product names, or titles unless the exact brand is required for a real technical reason.

## Use branded wording only when required

- Keep real filesystem paths unchanged when they are functional.
- Keep actual env vars, service names, container names, stream names, or URLs when they are real runtime contracts.
- If an example hostname or title is only illustrative, use a neutral placeholder instead of a project-specific brand.

## Why this matters

- Project names change more often than architecture rules.
- Brand-specific wording in AI artifacts creates instruction drift and makes reusable guidance look more concrete than it really is.
- Generic wording keeps the rules portable and focused on behavior instead of branding.

## Anti-patterns

- Putting the current project name in a rule or skill title when the title is really about architecture or workflow.
- Using a branded hostname in an example where any local hostname would work.
- Referring to `ProjectName conventions` when the rule is really `this project's conventions`.